                "text": "**Analyzing Grid Transitions**\n\nMy focus has shifted to understanding the transformations between input and output states, specifically how the grey shapes at the bottom are removed and replaced with blue elements. I'm observing the patterns of these changes to infer the underlying logic.\n\n**Observing Shape Transformations**\n\nI'm now focused on the numerical patterns, particularly the '1s' and '5s' in Example 1, trying to correlate them with the movement and transformation of shapes from the bottom. I suspect a rotation or flip is involved.\n\n**Confirming Vertical Flip**\n\nI've confirmed a vertical flip is occurring, noting how row 8 maps to row 2 and row 9 to row 3. I'm now investigating how shape 2 and the '0's transform into '1's in the output, noting specific cell coordinates.\n\n**Confirming Input Shape Manipulations**\n\nI've determined the input shapes are being vertically flipped. Now I'm cross-referencing the flipped shapes with their output positions, specifically investigating how Shape 1 (2x2) becomes the 2x2 output at (2,7)-(3,8) and how Shape 2 transforms.\n\n**Confirming Shape Orientation**\n\nI've confirmed that the input shapes are consistently undergoing a vertical flip. I'm now carefully mapping the specific coordinates of these flipped shapes to their final positions in the output, paying close attention to how shape components transform into their final representations.\n\n**Mapping Input to Output**\n\nI'm re-examining Example 2, specifically how a vertical domino at (8,5), (9,5) transforms into the output at (1,12), (2,12). I'm also analyzing a three-cell input shape at (8,7..8), (9,8) and its seemingly non-standard transformation into the output at (2,6..7), (3,7).\n\n**Translating Bottom Shapes**\n\nMy previous assumption of a flip was incorrect; the bottom shapes are simply being translated to the output. I've identified a consistent pattern where the input cell coordinates map directly to output coordinates